<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The UK economy grew faster than first esti=
mated in the second quarter of the year despite the disruption caused by th=
e Iran war.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">UK growth in April-June has been revised t=
o 0.5%, up from the previous estimate of 0.4%, in the latest national accou=
nts.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">That is a welcome piece of news for the ch=
ancellor, John Healey, as he draws up the budget due in four weeks=E2=80=99=
 time, and means his predecessor, Rachel Reeves, handed over a slightly lar=
ger economy than previously recognised.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Growth in Q2 2026 was driven by the servic=
es sector (where activity increased by 0.6%) and the construction sector (w=
hich grew by 0.8%), while the production sector shrank by 0.1%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">However, the Office for National Statistic=
s (ONS), which publishes the data, has also revised down its estimate for g=
rowth in 2025.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The ONS director of economic statistics Li=
z McKeown said: =E2=80=9CToday=E2=80=99s figures include our annual improve=
ments to the measurement of the economy, incorporating new information that=
 provides a better picture of activity across the UK=E2=80=99s service sect=
or, alongside the usual inclusion of updated and improved data sources.<br>=
<br>=E2=80=9CGrowth for 2025 as a whole was a little lower than previously =
estimated, with the profile of growth across the quarters also revised.<br>=
<br>=E2=80=9CHowever, stronger services growth in the latest quarter means =
the economy is now slightly larger than previously estimated.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The AI boom may have driven UK business in=
vestment up in the April-June quarter.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The ONS reports that business investment i=
s estimated to have increased by 1.8% in the quarter, and was 5.2% higher t=
han a year ago.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The UK bakery chain Greggs has announced p=
lans to cut more than 700 jobs despite a pickup in sales.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">It is proposing closing four of its produc=
tion sites, which could lead to up to 740 roles becoming redundant.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Greggs warns that with signs of greater in=
flationary pressures in 2027, it needs to cut costs to remain competitive.<=
/p>
